{"title":"What we are grateful for","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/974076359/0/marginalrevolution~What-we-are-grateful-for.html","date":1795695294000,"content":"<p>Here is <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.thefp.com/p/what-we-are-grateful-for-2026?utm_campaign=email-post&amp;r=3o9&amp;utm_source=substack&amp;utm_medium=email\" target=\"_blank\">The Free Press symposium</a>.  Here is an excerpt from my piece, which has a chance of turning out to be prescient by a day:</p>\r\n<blockquote><p>Since I was born in 1962, what has been the biggest fundamental theoretical breakthrough? We put a man on the moon and built the internet, but what about on a theoretical level? The standard model of particle physics, from the 1960s and 1970s, is one candidate. Still, the early 20th century was more exciting for theory.</p>\r\n<p>We still lack a theory of quantum gravity, and string theory looks less likely with time. The fundamentals of economics, my own field, have barely changed, even as our empirical knowledge has grown. Now I have more hope for some thrilling new theories, aided by both man and machine.</p></blockquote>\r\n<p>Just last night Ven Chandrasekaran and Astra dropped <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://x.com/kimmonismus/status/2125856219667566872\" target=\"_blank\">a new (and not yet confirmed) theory of quantum gravity</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/11/what-we-are-grateful-for.html\">What we are grateful for</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/974076359/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/974076359/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\